{% set contract %}
{% call m.line('trained', 'met') %}
Fit in the pipeline (`make recovery-bayes`) on the same {{ v('sim.weeks') }} weeks as own, and set beside own and the truth, channel by channel, as the cross check in the quarterly review.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('timed', 'met') %}
The panel runs from {{ v('sim.first_week') }} to {{ v('sim.last_week') }} and the manifest is as of {{ manifest.as_of }}; the fit sees no week after the panel's last, and the lift test it is calibrated on ran inside the panel, weeks {{ v('geo.start_week') }} to {{ v('geo.end_week') }}.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('calibrated', 'met') %}
Its {{ v('policy.interval_level') }} posterior intervals hold the truth in {{ v('mmm.bayes.covered_count') }} of {{ v('policy.channel_count') }} channels here and {{ v('recovery.bayes.coverage_overall') }} of the time across the recovery study; after lift calibration the tested channel's interval {{ 'covers' if raw('calibrate.bayes.covers_after') == 'yes' else 'misses' }} the truth, as the metrics say.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('useful', 'partly') %}
It informs the budget only through the cross check and the calibration comparison; the plan and the API use own's curves, so no dollar moves on bayes's estimates alone.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('fair', 'not applicable') %}
Not applicable to people, because the model sees weekly national spend and sales and no customer; the parties a plan affects are the channels and the teams behind them.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('gated', 'partly') %}
Each fit is checked against ceilings on divergences and R hat set in `marginal_mmm.interface` and marked failed when it crosses them (a test checks this); the status is reported, not raised, so a failed fit would not stop the pipeline, and every fit in this build is marked ok.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('served', 'not served') %}
The API image is built without the bayes extra and no route fits or reads bayes curves; `/v1/models` lists the calibrated bayes export only as a file on disk.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('integrated', 'met') %}
The site's Mix page sets its returns and curves beside own's and the truth, and its recovery fits are the "before" of the bayes calibration study.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('monitored', 'not built') %}
There is no scheduled refit or drift check, and the weekly refresh is a stretch goal; the recovery study's fits are logged in `logs/recovery_bayes.log`.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('documented', 'met') %}
This card, `docs/models.md` and `docs/evaluation.md`, all rendered from the manifest by the claim gate.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('bounded', 'met') %}
The statement closes this card: the brand is fictional and the market simulated, so every return here describes the simulator.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('validated', 'met') %}
Graded against the simulator's known truth, never against itself: {{ v('recovery.bayes.fits') }} recovery fits across {{ v('recovery.bayes.conditions') }} conditions at {{ v('recovery.bayes.seeds_per_condition') }} seeds each, and the truth beside every estimate on the demonstration brand.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('explainable at the decision', 'met') %}
A reader sees each channel's return with its posterior interval and the truth beside own's, and the response curves on the Mix page.
{% endcall %}
{% endset %}
{% set limitations %}
The recovery study rests on {{ v('recovery.bayes.seeds_per_condition') }} seeds per condition, because
each fit samples for minutes; its per condition figures are indicative and the order of the
conditions could change with more seeds.

The demonstration fit ran {{ v('mmm.bayes.chains') }} chains, fewer than PyMC recommends for a
reliable R hat, and most recovery fits reported a few divergences and an R hat above PyMC's warning
level while staying under the backend's ceilings. More chains and a higher target acceptance are
the first things to change if the study is rerun.

The baseline is additive where the simulator's demand is multiplicative, so some of the recovery
error may be the cost of that choice rather than of the sampler.

Calibration narrows the tested channel's interval until it follows one experiment, and that
experiment sits {{ 'above' if raw('geo.did.estimate') > raw('geo.truth') else 'below' }} the truth: the
calibrated interval {{ 'misses' if raw('calibrate.bayes.covers_after') == 'no' else 'covers' }} the true
return. One experiment is not enough to make that interval trustworthy.

It is not served, so nothing a visitor to the API does uses it.
{% endset %}
